          question: 'What is the difference between PIPEDA and Quebec\'s Law 25?',
          answer: 'PIPEDA (federal) applies to the collection, use, and disclosure of personal information in the course of commercial activities by private sector organisations across Canada. It requires: consent for collection, use, and disclosure of personal information (with limited exceptions), notification of breach within 72 hours of determining a breach poses a real risk of significant harm, and individual rights to access and correct personal information. Quebec\'s Law 25 (an Act to modernise legislative provisions as regards the protection of personal information) is more stringent than PIPEDA and is modelled after GDPR. It adds: mandatory privacy by design for all new technology products, data protection impact assessments (DPIAs) for projects involving personal information, right to data portability (in a technology-neutral structured format), right to be forgotten (subject to exceptions), appointment of a Privacy Officer, and a 60-day breach notification requirement. Quebec\'s Law 25 applies to all organisations that collect personal information from Quebec residents, regardless of where the organisation is located. ClickMasters implements both PIPEDA and Quebec Law 25 compliance for all Canadian client engagements.',
